; lib/secret.chiral -- the minimal custody primitive (E40, Bucket-2 proof).
;
; Secret is a porttype: an opaque linear atom, exactly like Sock/Fd in
; ports.chiral. No data constructor exists, so no `case` can prise out its
; bytes, and the kernel holds every caller to move-only (quantity-1) threading.
; The plaintext bytes are reachable ONLY through `secret-reveal`, so the one
; place plaintext re-enters the program is the single greppable name below.
;
; Honest limit: once revealed the Bytes are ordinary and untracked. This
; reduces the leak surface to named, auditable exits; it does not prove the
; revealed bytes never reach a socket (that stronger property is information-
; flow labelling, E44, deferred). No crypto here: seal takes already-plaintext
; bytes -- sealing is custody, not encryption.

(import "prelude/prelude")   ; Bytes, Unit
(import "ports/ports")     ; Sock, sock-send

; ---- the sealed type -------------------------------------------------------
; Opaque linear atom. No constructor exists, so no `case` can prise out its
; bytes; the kernel holds every caller to move-only threading automatically.
(porttype Secret)

; reveal's result shape, mirroring ports.chiral's RecvR/AccR/PoolR: every port
; result there is a data wrapper, and that is not decoration -- the wrapper is
; how an unrestricted value escapes a linear effect. `bs` is an unannotated
; (unrestricted, w) field, so the plaintext that comes out of a case on a
; RevealR is ordinary Bytes that ordinary sinks accept, WITHOUT dragging the
; Secret's linearity along. A revealed secret returning bare `Bytes` cannot
; feed sock-send (whose Bytes slot is w) without forcing the linear Secret to w
; too -- the wrapper is what makes the exit expressible.
(data RevealR () (reveal-r (bs Bytes)))

; ---- the ONLY operations that mention Secret --------------------------------
; seal: wrap already-plaintext Bytes into a Secret. Effectful: a category-C
; crossing that mints a capability-shaped value.
(extern secret-seal   (=> Bytes Secret))

; reveal: the single guarded exit. Consumes the Secret (linear) and yields the
; plaintext inside a RevealR -- THIS is the one place Bytes come back, audit by
; grepping the one name. The host binding zeroes its own copy on the way out.
(extern secret-reveal (=> (1 s Secret) RevealR))

; wipe: discharge a Secret you decide not to use. Linearity forbids dropping it
; silently, so this is how a Secret leaves scope without ever being revealed.
(extern secret-wipe   (=> (1 s Secret) Unit))

; ---- the DEMO twin: compiles, because it uses the exit -----------------------
; The leak `(sock-send s pw)` does NOT type-check: sock-send wants Bytes in its
; second slot and a Secret is not Bytes (see demo/passman-min.chiral for the
; rejected case). Legal path: reveal consumes the Secret and hands its plaintext
; out through the one audited exit; casing the RevealR binds the ordinary Bytes,
; which the stock sink then accepts. Consumes pw (in reveal) and s (in send).
(def send-revealed (=> (1 pw Secret) (1 s Sock) SendR)
  (lam (pw s)
    (do (<- r (secret-reveal pw))         ; the single audited Secret -> RevealR
        (case r ((reveal-r bs) (sock-send s bs))))))
